Dear All,

Like many on here I am fairly new to unmarked and would like to estimate a detectability corrected index of abundance (not necessarily true abundance) using the occuRN model. These estimates will ultimately be used in a piece wise SEM similar to (Cunningham et al. 2020) to similarly determine impacts of mesopredators release/landscape of fear/trophic cascade affects in a tropical ecosystem.

However, my study design is using old data not tailored for this purpose as a result of COVID. As such I have a limited number of camera trapping grids (10 or 11) compared to the study mentioned above (28) making it difficult to create reliable models for the SEM. I was wondering if would be possible to use individual camera traps as opposed to camera grids as I have around 485 independent cameras split between the grids over a period of several years. Is there some reason not to do this if its the difference between 10 data points and 485?

I initially tried modeling the index of abundance using:
M1 <- occuRN(~ effort ~ Cam_Grid, data = camtrapR_Frame)
M2 <- occuRN(~ effort + Forest_Road + Ridge ~ Cam_Grid, data = camtrapR_Frame)
M3 <- occuRN(~ effort ~ Cam_station, data = camtrapR_Frame)
ect.

I have read the post "RN model to estimate abundance per site" which seems to imply I could  interpret "abundance" as intensity of habitat use based on different covariates but not compare them. Would this still hold true if these estimates were then compared in the models used to construct the piece wise SEMs? Additionally I am not sure if taking this approach would subsequently require the use of GLMMs in the SEM step...

Any insight would be apricated and I apologize if I'm missing something obvious.

Best,
Darwin

